WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.839
In this module, we are going to focus on Model Context Protocol.

2
00:00:03.839 --> 00:00:09.039
MCP is a communication layer designed to provide Cloud with context and tools

3
00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:12.720
without requiring you, the developer, to write a bunch of tedious code.

4
00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:16.799
When you first get started with MCP, you will see diagrams that look like this

5
00:00:16.799 --> 00:00:22.559
very often. It shows two major elements of MCP, namely the client and the server.

6
00:00:22.559 --> 00:00:25.920
The server often contains a number of internal components,

7
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.319
named tools, resources, and prompts. There's a lot of terminology here,

8
00:00:30.319 --> 00:00:35.040
so to help you understand all of this, we're going to imagine that we are building a small app

9
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:40.000
and see how MCP fits into it. Our sample app is going to be another

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.439
chat interface. It's going to allow a user to chat with Cloud about their GitHub data.

11
00:00:45.439 --> 00:00:50.080
So if a user asks a question like, what open pull request do I have across all my different

12
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.040
repositories, the expectation is that Cloud is probably going to make use of a tool

13
00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.000
to reach out to GitHub, access the user's account, and see what open pull requests they have,

14
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.879
maybe open repositories or whatever else. The point here is that we would implement this

15
00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:09.040
probably by using a set of tools. Now one thing I want to mention

16
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.279
really quickly is that GitHub has a tremendous amount of functionality.

17
00:01:13.279 --> 00:01:18.639
There are repositories, pull requests, issues, projects, and tons of other things.

18
00:01:19.199 --> 00:01:24.720
So to have a complete GitHub chatbot, we would really have to author a tremendous number of

19
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.519
tools. If we wanted to build that sample app, we would be on the hook for authoring all these

20
00:01:29.519 --> 00:01:34.800
schemas and all these functions. And this is all code that you and I as developers would have to

21
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.239
write, test, and maintain. That's a lot of effort, a lot of burden being placed on us.

22
00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:46.239
This challenge of making developers maintain a big set of integrations is one of the primary

23
00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:53.360
difficulties that model context aims to solve. MCP shifts the burden of defining and running tools

24
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.959
from your server to something else called an MCP server. So no longer would you and I have to

25
00:01:58.959 --> 00:02:04.080
author this tool right here. Instead, it would be authored and executed somewhere else inside

26
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.600
of this MCP server. These MCP servers can really be thought of as like an interface to some outside

27
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.679
service. So I might have a GitHub MCP server that provides access to data and functionality provided

28
00:02:15.679 --> 00:02:20.880
by specifically GitHub. We're essentially wrapping up a ton of functionality around GitHub and

29
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.479
placing it into this MCP server in the form of a set of tools. So at this point, we have a very

30
00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:32.080
basic understanding of what a MCP server is. It gives us access to a set of tools that exposes

31
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.440
functionality related to some outside service. And the benefit here is that you and I do not

32
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.720
have to author all these different tool schemas and functions and so on. Now that we have this

33
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.759
basic understanding, I want to address some very common questions that a lot of people have when

34
00:02:47.759 --> 00:02:52.800
they first learn about MCP servers. So three common questions that seem to always come up.

35
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.240
The first common question is who authors these MCP servers? And the answer is anyone. Anyone can

36
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.919
make an MCP server implementation. But very often, you will find that service providers make their own

37
00:03:03.919 --> 00:03:09.279
official implementation. So for example, AWS might decide to release their own official

38
00:03:09.279 --> 00:03:14.559
MCP server implementation. And inside of it, it might have a wide variety of different tools

39
00:03:14.559 --> 00:03:21.119
available for you to use. The second common question is how is using a MCP server different

40
00:03:21.119 --> 00:03:27.919
than just calling a services API directly? Well, as we just saw, if we wanted to call a API directly,

41
00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:37.600
such as GitHub, then we would have to author this tool ourselves. And now we can call GitHub directly.

42
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.800
So what did we gain here? Well, all that really changed was we are now having to author the schema

43
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.479
ourselves and the function implementation ourselves. So simply by adding in the MCP server,

44
00:03:48.479 --> 00:03:53.520
we are saving ourselves a little bit of time. The final common question is more of a common

45
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.559
criticism that you're going to see people have around MCP. And this criticism is most often

46
00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:03.119
coming from people who don't quite understand what MCP is all about. So very often, you will

47
00:04:03.119 --> 00:04:08.479
see people saying MCP and tool use are the same thing. Well, as I have just laid out to you,

48
00:04:08.479 --> 00:04:13.119
MCP servers and tool use, they are complementary. They are different things, but they are

49
00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:19.279
complementary. The idea behind MCP is that you do not have to author the tool function and the tool

50
00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:23.920
schema. That is something that's done for you by someone else and is being wrapped up inside of

51
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:29.600
this MCP server. So at some level, yeah, they're kind of similar because we are talking about tool

52
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.559
use in both cases. But MCP servers are really talking about who is doing the actual work.

53
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.279
So if you ever see this criticism, again,

54
00:04:37.279 --> 00:04:40.799
it's usually because people don't quite understand what MCP is all about.